// specs.memql
//
// Common-namespace specs. Under the spec/shape binding redesign (epic
// #2281) a caller / authorization predicate is a `spec` bound to an
// @actor shape in its signature; it reads the projected envelope key by
// bare name and evaluates in-process (context-spec).
//
// Callers name it as a bare top-level conjunct in a filter -- that is
// what the live corpus does and what the per-row-authz admin gate
// recognises. The `spec("name")` stringly form this header used to
// prescribe is RETIRED: component/memql/ast_converter.go rejects it and
// names the predicate form `spec <name>` (no quotes, no parens) as its
// replacement (memql#2983).
//
// ===========================================================================
// A ROLE COMPARISON IS NOT ONE OF THESE ANY MORE (epic memql#5166, D11)
// ===========================================================================
//
// This file held three -- `requiresAdmin`, `requiresOwnerOrAdmin` and
// `requiresDeveloperOrAbove` -- and all three are deleted. They compared the
// actor's role STRING against one or three literals, and that shape has three
// faults the annotations do not:
//
//   - it cannot see a custom role AT ALL. `actor.role == "admin"` is false for a
//     rank-250 role holding every principal verb, so every role a cluster
//     authored for itself was refused every surface these gated, whatever it
//     held. That is the defect epic memql#5166 exists to end.
//   - the names went stale silently. `requiresDeveloperOrAbove` reads as a
//     floor and WAS a three-value set; its own comment admitted the name
//     "predates the ladder flip and reads a rung too high".
//   - a misspelled spec name is a missing conjunct nothing notices. dslgate's
//     AdminGateRe recognises gates BY NAME, so a gate it does not know is not
//     a gate, and the composition rule never runs on a filter using one.
//
// The replacements say which question they are asking. `@requiresRank("x")` is
// a FLOOR on the cluster's ladder; `@requiresCapability("verb", "resource")` is
// a GRANT a role was given. Both are validated at LOAD -- a typo refuses boot
// rather than gating on nothing -- and both are enforced at execution, on the
// direct call and on every plan that expands the construct.
//
// What still belongs here is a caller predicate that is NOT about the role
// ladder: a check over the projected actor envelope that no rank and no grant
// can express. There is none today, which is why this file declares nothing.
